D['dhairya']=dict(desc='Dhairya Lakṣmī (Vīra Lakṣmī), Lakṣmī of courage: her red, eight-armed warrior form with discus, conch, bow and sword; courage as the hidden wealth; Sītā, Sāvitrī and Prahlāda as models; her link with Durgā and Navarātri.',
 tag='Lakṣmī of courage',fwq=("Courage is wealth?","Lose money, you can earn it back. Lose courage, you lose everything."),
 fw=['<b>Dhairya</b> means courage, steadiness, fortitude. Dhairya Lakṣmī, also called <b>Vīra Lakṣmī</b>, “Lakṣmī of the brave”, is the Goddess as inner strength: the wealth that lets us face illness, loss and danger without breaking.',
     'Unlike the gentle Lakṣmī on her lotus, she is shown as a warrior with weapons in eight hands. This guide asks why fortune needs courage, and finds the answer in the great steadfast figures of the epics.'],
 pages=[('glance','AT A GLANCE','Dhairya Lakṣmī on one page',[('Name','Dhairya Lakṣmī, also Vīra Lakṣmī: “Lakṣmī of courage”'),('Gift','Courage, steadiness and endurance in hard times'),('Form','Red sari; eight arms with discus, conch, bow, arrow, trident or sword, a book, and boon and fear-not gestures'),('Feel','The warrior Lakṣmī, close to Durgā'),('Prayed to','In crises: illness, debt, danger, grief')],("Why does a Lakṣmī carry weapons?","To protect what she gives. And to give us the nerve to protect it ourselves.")),
  ('dhairya','WHAT DHAIRYA MEANS','More than bravery',[('Dhairya','Steadiness, fortitude: holding firm over time'),('Vīrya','Valour in the moment of danger'),('Dhṛti','The Gītā’s word for steadfast will (18.33–35)'),('Together','Vīra Lakṣmī covers both: the brave act and the long endurance')]),
  ('durga','CLOSE TO DURGĀ','The warrior Goddess',[('Weapons','Like Durgā, she holds the discus, conch and bow given by the gods'),('Devī Māhātmya','There the great Goddess is called Mahālakṣmī in the middle episode, slaying Mahiṣāsura'),('Navarātri','The middle three nights are often given to Lakṣmī'),('One power','Courage and fortune as one Śakti')],None,'<div class="key">See: <a href="/bhakti/devi/devi-mahatmya/">Devī Māhātmya</a> · <a href="/bhakti/devi/mahishasura-mardini/">Mahiṣāsuramardinī</a></div>'),
  ('sita','SĪTĀ IN THE AŚOKA GROVE','Courage without weapons',[('Captive','Alone in Laṅkā for months, threatened daily'),('The grass','She spoke to Rāvaṇa through a blade of grass'),('Refusal','She refused Hanumān’s offer of escape, so Rāma could win her back'),('Sītā is Lakṣmī','The epic’s model of dhairya is Lakṣmī herself')],("No sword, no army?","Just a blade of grass and a will no one could bend.")),
  ('savitri','SĀVITRĪ AND PRAHLĀDA','Steadfast against death and a tyrant',[('Sāvitrī','In the Mahābhārata she follows Yama, god of death, and argues until he returns her husband Satyavān'),('Prahlāda','A boy who would not stop praying, though his father tried to kill him'),('Common thread','Neither fought; both held firm'),('Reward','Yama’s boons; Narasiṃha’s appearance')]),
  ('stotram','HER VERSE','In the Aṣṭalakṣmī Stotram',[('Theme','Her verse praises her as the remover of fear, the giver of wisdom and courage'),('Tone','Its words are bolder, like a war-cry'),('Refrain','It ends asking her to protect the singer always'),('When','Many chant it alone before exams, surgery or travel')]),
  ('worship','HOW SHE IS WORSHIPPED','Prayers for courage',[('Day','Friday, and Tuesday in some homes'),('Red','Red flowers and kumkum'),('With Durgā','In Navarātri and on Ayudha Pūjā'),('Practice','A few lines of courage before any hard task')]),
  ('daily','COURAGE IN DAILY LIFE','Ordinary dhairya',[('Patients','Facing illness and treatment'),('Parents','Holding a family together through loss'),('Students','Trying again after failure'),('Honesty','Speaking truth when silence is easier')],("Is that really Lakṣmī?","The texts say yes. Fortune stays with the steady.")),
  ('meaning','WHAT SHE TEACHES','The hidden wealth',[('Keep your nerve','Fortune comes and goes; steadiness keeps you whole'),('Protect what matters','Strength serves the good'),('Courage is quiet','Often it is just not giving up'),('Ask for it','It is fine to pray for courage')]),
 ],
 end=[('Who','Lakṣmī of courage, also Vīra Lakṣmī'),('Form','Eight-armed, red, armed like Durgā'),('Models','Sītā, Sāvitrī, Prahlāda'),('Worship','Red flowers; Navarātri; before hard tasks'),('Lesson','Steadiness is the wealth that keeps all others')],endq=("One line?","Money can be lost and found. Courage is the wealth that keeps the rest."),
 src='Bhagavad Gītā 18.33–35 (dhṛti); Devī Māhātmya (Mahālakṣmī episode); Vālmīki Rāmāyaṇa, Sundara Kāṇḍa; Mahābhārata, Vana Parva (Sāvitrī); Bhāgavata Purāṇa 7 (Prahlāda); Aṣṭalakṣmī temple iconography.',
 unv='Her weapons vary between images; which nights of Navarātri belong to Lakṣmī differs by region.',
 EX={'glance':['Dhairya Lakṣmī is the most surprising of the eight. Fortune, we might think, is about getting things; but tradition says that without courage, nothing gained can be kept. She is the steady hand on the tiller.','Her name Vīra Lakṣmī is also used for royal fortune: the courage of a king in war. In some lists she is paired with Vijaya Lakṣmī, victory, the fruit of courage.'],
  'dhairya':['Sanskrit has many words for courage. Dhairya, from dhīra, “firm, wise, steady”, suggests a calm, long-lasting strength rather than a burst of heroism. The Gītā praises the dhīra, who is not shaken by pleasure or pain (2.15).','In the Gītā’s analysis, dhṛti, steadfastness, comes in three kinds: sāttvika, holding the mind and senses to the good; rājasika, holding to gain; and tāmasika, holding stubbornly to fear and sleep. Dhairya Lakṣmī is the first.'],
  'durga':['The Devī Māhātmya, which we cover in full, has three episodes: in the second, the Goddess who kills the buffalo demon is called Mahālakṣmī, made from the combined powers of all the gods. So a warrior Lakṣmī is not a modern idea at all.','In many South Indian homes Navarātri is divided into three sets of three nights: Durgā, Lakṣmī and Sarasvatī. Dhairya Lakṣmī stands at the meeting point of the first two.'],
  'sita':['Sītā’s courage is the model of dhairya in the Indian imagination. Her captivity in the Aśoka grove, as described in Vālmīki’s Sundara Kāṇḍa, is a long study in endurance: threats, temptations and despair, met with refusal.','Since Sītā is Lakṣmī in human form, the epic in effect shows Lakṣmī herself practising the virtue her eight-armed form represents.'],
  'savitri':['Sāvitrī’s story, told in the Mahābhārata’s Vana Parva, is still honoured in the Vaṭa Sāvitrī vrata. She follows Yama after he takes her husband’s life, and her calm, clever arguments win boon after boon until Yama must return Satyavān.','Prahlāda, the boy devotee of the Bhāgavata Purāṇa, survives poison, snakes, elephants and fire, holding to his devotion until Narasiṃha appears from a pillar. Both stories show courage as persistence rather than force.'],
  'stotram':['Her verse in the Aṣṭalakṣmī Stotram has a martial rhythm, with words like drums and war-cries, in keeping with her form. Many people chant it alone, separate from the full hymn, when facing something frightening.','As with the others, we describe it rather than reproduce it.'],
  'daily':['Courage in everyday life rarely looks heroic. It is the patient going in for treatment, the widow raising children alone, the student sitting an exam again, the employee who reports a wrong.','Tradition’s point is that these too are Lakṣmī: a form of wealth no one can steal.'],
  'meaning':['Dhairya Lakṣmī teaches that inner wealth guards outer wealth. Without steadiness, money is spent in panic, opportunities are lost to fear, and families fall apart in crisis.','Praying for courage is not weakness. Many of the great figures of the epics asked for it, and the tradition gave it a Goddess.']})
D['gaja']=dict(desc='Gaja Lakṣmī, Lakṣmī with the elephants: the oldest image of the Goddess, bathed by two elephants, from the Śrī Sūkta, Bharhut, Sāñcī and Udayagiri to Indo-Scythian coins, Ellora and Hoysaḷa door lintels; royal power, rain and abundance.',
 tag='Lakṣmī with the elephants',fwq=("Why elephants?","Rain-clouds, kings' power and the churning of the ocean, all at once."),
 fw=['<b>Gaja Lakṣmī</b> is the Goddess on a lotus while two elephants pour water over her from pots held in their trunks. It is the oldest surviving image of Lakṣmī, carved more than two thousand years ago, and still the one above countless temple doors.',
     'This guide follows the image through Vedic poetry, Buddhist and Jain stūpas, ancient coins and Karnataka’s temple doorways, and asks what the elephants mean.'],
 pages=[('glance','AT A GLANCE','Gaja Lakṣmī on one page',[('Name','Gaja Lakṣmī, “Lakṣmī with elephants”; also Abhiṣeka Lakṣmī'),('Gift','Royal power, rain and abundance'),('Form','Seated or standing on a lotus, holding lotuses; two (sometimes four) elephants pour water on her'),('Age','Among the oldest Indian goddess images, from the 2nd century BCE'),('Seen','Above temple doors, on coins, on stūpa gateways')],("Two elephants giving a bath?","A royal bath. It's how kings were crowned.")),
  ('srisukta','IN THE ŚRĪ SŪKTA','Delighting in elephants',[('Hastināda-pramodinī','She “delights in the trumpeting of elephants”'),('Aśvapūrvām rathamadhyām','“With horses before her, in the middle of chariots”: a royal procession'),('Padme sthitām','“Standing on a lotus”'),('Root','The hymn already contains the Gaja Lakṣmī picture')],None,'<div class="verse"><p><i>aśvapūrvāṃ rathamadhyāṃ hastinādapramodinīm<br>śriyaṃ devīm upahvaye śrīr mā devī juṣatām</i></p><p class="small">I call on Śrī: horses before her, chariots around her, delighting in the elephants’ call. May the Goddess Śrī be pleased with me.</p></div>'),
  ('churning','THE OCEAN’S BATH','From the churning',[('Rising','When Lakṣmī rose from the ocean of milk, the elephants of the directions bathed her'),('Golden pots','They poured the waters of sacred rivers from golden jars'),('Then','She garlanded Viṣṇu'),('Source','Viṣṇu Purāṇa 1.9 and the Bhāgavata Purāṇa 8.8')]),
  ('stupas','ON THE STŪPAS','Buddhist and Jain Gaja Lakṣmī',[('Bharhut','Railings, 2nd century BCE: Gaja Lakṣmī among the earliest carvings'),('Sāñcī','The great stūpa’s gateways, 1st century BCE, carry her many times'),('Udayagiri','Odisha’s Jain caves (Rānī Gumphā) show her too'),('Jain dreams','Mother Triśalā’s auspicious dreams before Mahāvīra’s birth include Lakṣmī anointed by elephants'),('Shared','Before sects, she was a sign of good fortune for all')],("On a Buddhist stūpa?","Good luck belongs to everyone. Buddhists and Jains used her too.")),
  ('coins','ON ANCIENT COINS','Fortune in the purse',[('Kauśāmbī and Ujjain','Early punch-marked and cast coins of north India show her'),('Azilises','Indo-Scythian king of the 1st century BCE: Gaja Lakṣmī on his coins'),('Meaning','A king’s fortune and the land’s prosperity on money itself'),('Continuity','Lakṣmī on coins continued under the Guptas and later dynasties')]),
  ('lintels','ABOVE THE DOOR','Lalāṭa-bimba',[('Lalāṭa-bimba','The small image on the centre of a temple door lintel'),('Gaja Lakṣmī','Very often she is the one, blessing all who enter'),('Karnataka','Common on Hoysaḷa and Vijayanagara doorways, and on house doors'),('Ellora','A great Gaja Lakṣmī panel at the Kailāsa temple, 8th century')],("Why on the door?","So that everyone who walks in passes under her blessing.")),
  ('meaning1','THE ELEPHANTS','Rain, kingship, water',[('Rain-clouds','Elephants are linked with clouds; their bath is rain on the fields'),('Kings','Elephants were royal animals; the abhiṣeka bath crowns a king'),('Directions','The eight diggajas hold up the world'),('Water','Fortune comes as a flow, not a hoard')]),
  ('stotram','HER VERSE','In the Aṣṭalakṣmī Stotram',[('Theme','Her verse pictures her with elephants, horses and chariots, as in the Śrī Sūkta'),('Royal','It calls her the giver of a kingdom’s splendour'),('Refrain','It asks her to protect the singer always'),('Use','Sung at house-warmings and door rituals')]),
  ('worship','HOW SHE IS WORSHIPPED','In home and temple',[('Doorways','Many homes carve or paint her above the main door'),('Abhiṣeka','Temple bathing rites echo her elephant bath'),('Varalakṣmī','Her image is common on the kalaśa decorations'),('Elephants','Temple elephants bless devotees with their trunks')]),
  ('meaning','WHAT SHE TEACHES','Fortune flows',[('Abundance is shared','Water poured on her flows to everyone'),('Power with grace','Elephant strength bowing to the lotus'),('Welcome at the door','Begin every entry with blessing'),('Ancient and common','One image for Hindus, Buddhists and Jains alike')]),
 ],
 end=[('Who','Lakṣmī bathed by elephants'),('Roots','Śrī Sūkta; the churning'),('History','Bharhut, Sāñcī, Udayagiri, Indo-Scythian coins, Ellora'),('Doors','Above temple and house doors'),('Lesson','Fortune flows like water: share it')],endq=("One line?","The oldest picture of Lakṣmī is a bath: fortune pouring down for everyone."),
 src='Śrī Sūkta; Viṣṇu Purāṇa 1.9; Bhāgavata Purāṇa 8.8; art-historical surveys of Bharhut, Sāñcī and Udayagiri–Khaṇḍagiri; numismatic catalogues for Azilises’ Gaja Lakṣmī coins; Ellora Cave 16 (Kailāsa).',
 unv='Exact dates for early sites are approximate; identification of every early female figure with elephants as Lakṣmī is accepted by most but not all scholars.',
 EX={'glance':['Gaja Lakṣmī may be the single most widespread image in Indian art. It appears on Buddhist railings, Jain caves, Hindu temples, coins, seals, textiles, wedding cards and house doors. Its meaning was clear to all: abundance, rain and royal good fortune.','Because the image is so old and so shared, Gaja Lakṣmī is also a reminder that much of Indian religious imagery grew from a common stock before the traditions separated.'],
  'srisukta':['The Śrī Sūkta’s third verse, given here, sets the scene of a royal procession: horses in front, chariots in the middle, elephants trumpeting. The Goddess is called to come with all this splendour.','Elsewhere the hymn places her on a lotus, lotus-coloured and lotus-scented. The elephant-bath picture combines these elements.'],
  'churning':['In the Bhāgavata Purāṇa’s telling, the sages perform Lakṣmī’s abhiṣeka as she rises, the rivers bring their waters, and the elephants of the quarters pour them from golden pots. It is a coronation scene, with the Goddess as queen of the universe.','Gaja Lakṣmī thus also commemorates Lakṣmī’s return to the world after Durvāsā’s curse.'],
  'stupas':['At Bharhut and Sāñcī, Gaja Lakṣmī appears among the carved medallions and on the architraves of the gateways. Scholars debate whether Buddhists saw her as a goddess or as a symbol, perhaps even of the Buddha’s mother Māyā; either way she meant good fortune.','In Jain tradition, the mothers of the Tīrthaṅkaras see fourteen (or sixteen) auspicious dreams before the birth; one of them is Śrī, anointed by elephants. Jain manuscripts painted this scene for centuries.'],
  'coins':['Coins of Kauśāmbī and Ujjain, among the earliest Indian coinages, show a goddess with elephants. Azilises, an Indo-Scythian ruler of the 1st century BCE, put Gaja Lakṣmī on some of his coins, showing how foreign rulers adopted Indian symbols of royal fortune.','Lakṣmī remained a coin image for centuries, most famously on Gupta gold coins, where she sits on a lotus or a throne.'],
  'lintels':['The lalāṭa-bimba, “forehead image”, is the small figure at the centre of a door lintel. In Śiva temples it may be Gaṇeśa, in Viṣṇu temples Garuḍa or Viṣṇu, but Gaja Lakṣmī appears across all kinds of temples and in houses.','In Karnataka, house doors carved with Gaja Lakṣmī are still made for new homes, especially in older towns and villages.'],
  'meaning1':['The elephants carry layered meanings. In Indian poetry they are compared to rain-clouds, dark and heavy, so their bath is the monsoon on the fields. They were also the mounts of kings, and the abhiṣeka, a sacred bath, is the core of a royal consecration.','The eight elephants of the directions, the diggajas, support the earth in Purāṇic cosmology. When they bathe Lakṣmī, the whole world honours her.'],
  'stotram':['Her verse draws directly on the Śrī Sūkta’s picture of elephants, horses and chariots. It is a royal verse, about splendour and dignity as much as money.','We describe rather than reproduce it.'],
  'worship':['Gaja Lakṣmī is worshipped less as a separate deity than as a blessing image. Painting her over the door at a gṛhapraveśa, house-warming, is a common custom.','In temples with elephants, the elephant’s trunk on a devotee’s head is itself felt as Gaja Lakṣmī’s blessing.'],
  'meaning':['The image teaches that fortune is a flow, like water poured from a height, not a heap to be guarded. The water runs off the Goddess and onto the ground, for all.','Its long history across faiths also shows that a good symbol needs no gatekeeper.']})
for i,k in [(2,'dhairya'),(3,'gaja')]: FORM(i,D[k])
